        "title": "The curse of the referee",
        "text": "It is hard to understand why people become referees. From American football to baseball, from ice hockey to polo, referees have been shouted at, sworn at, spat at, pushed, punched and kicked. Their eyesight has been questioned and their changing rooms have been damaged. Certainly, nobody becomes a referee to win a popularity contest.\n\nAnd yet most of them carry on putting up with the abuse, along with their small financial reward, and give up their free time purely for the love of the game. To reach the top as a football referee – and qualify for an allowance of around £300 for each Premier League match – takes at least 15 years.\n\nIt is not an easy job to do well. Referees have to take written and oral examinations, and they also have to pass strict eyesight tests. Their performance and fitness are constantly monitored. Because it takes so long to rise through the ranks, most of the top referees are in their forties, but they will run between 10 km and 15 km during a 90-minute match, much of it backwards.\n\nCricket umpires do not need the fitness of football or rugby referees, but their job is no less demanding. They have to stand for hours in blazing sunshine and make instant decisions about something that happens 20 m away as a result of a ball thrown at 145 km an hour. That takes an experienced eye.\n\nTennis umpires face similar problems, although they have the advantage of doing their job sitting down. And at the top level, they now have the help of modern technology in the form of 'Cyclops', the electronic eye on the service line.\n\nUnlike cricket umpires, tennis umpires also have to keep the score and run several stopwatches at once. There are now limits on the length of time between games, points, first and second serves, and for assessing and treating injuries. In addition, they have to rule on 'racket abuse' (in other words, outbursts of bad temper) and give regular warnings to spectators against using mobile phones or flash photography. Their only reward is a quick handshake at the end of the match.\n\nEvery sport presents its own problems for referees. The rules of American football run to 250 pages, and it is the only sport in which referees use a microphone to explain their decisions to the crowd. Water polo referees have a particular disadvantage: they stand beside the pool, although 75 per cent of fouls are committed under water.\n\nBowls referees have to make sure that none of the players is wearing jeans. Putting talcum powder on the soles of shoes, to help the foot slide smoothly when the ball is released, is also forbidden.\n\nReferees of physical sports, especially rugby, boxing and ice hockey, must be brave. A boxing referee has to protect the losing fighter from further punishment and be ready to throw himself into the fight. Think, too, of the sumo wrestling judges, who must get close enough to recognise a legal hold and be quick enough not to be crushed under a 300 kg mountain of a man.\n\nRefereeing is a dirty (and sometimes dangerous) job. Referees and umpires will never be loved. As the football manager Ron Atkinson said after his team was knocked out of a European Cup match in controversial circumstances: 'I never comment on referees and I'm not going to break the habit of a lifetime for that twerp.'"
